WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.860
Mr. Rigmaiden was a man that . . . like when they’D have meetings like the police jury . . . You know we had to vote.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.800
So they would ask Mr. Rigmaiden, his name was James Rigmaiden.

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.640
And then there was his father before him.

4
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.240
But it was either the Rigmaidens or Coach William and them, the Mosses.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.920
They were the people that mainly dealt with a lot of the business part of Mossville, you know.

6
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.780
It was a lot of them that got involved in it.

7
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.580
Because it was my daddy, Mr. Moss, Mr. Rigmaiden, and the Williams.

8
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.900
They were the people that, you know, when they wanted something to be talked about,

9
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.880
or politicians came in the neighborhood, they knew who to go to.

10
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:52.040
They knew who had the pull, so those four families right there . Every time you had to vote, you know, the politicians would come out.

11
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.360
That's the only time you’d see them is when they wanted you to vote for them.

12
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.100
“If you get some votes for me,” you know.

13
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.860
“Make sure your kids sign up to vote,” and stuff like that.

14
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.800
An old man named Mr. Montgomery.

15
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.600
Valery Montgomery.

16
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.780
They get to . . . Just by name, people are like, "Who can I talk to?"

17
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:14.720
They come in the neighborhood, "Who you think I could talk to can help me win this vote?"

18
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.340
That's the kind of game they did.

19
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:23.640
Before you know it, they would pull this one here on Mr. Rigmaiden or Mr. Montgomery and before you know it,

20
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.020
boy, them old men were on they job for these people.

21
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.520
And beating us across the head.

22
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.440
"You got to vote!

23
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.760
And you got to vote for him!"

24
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:34.480
That's the kind of stuff . . . how it all got started.

25
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.600
Did Edwin Edwards ever come to the community?

26
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.760
Yes.

27
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.080
These older people knew all of these politicians.

28
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:48.660
There was not one politician that came up . . . [James J.] Cox, and Edwin Edwards, and everybody you knew.

29
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.940
These older people [snaps] were like brothers with them and they come to they house and sit down and eat with them.

30
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.800
We all knew them.

31
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.800
They knew how to play on the older people.

32
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:07.540
It went down line, because those same politicians that had family members would come back to us as younger people.

33
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.660
They still come to us.

34
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.460
Now Mr. Cox, he's still with us.

35
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.060
"Oh I knew your father."

36
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.660
Of course you did.

37
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:15.080
You knew it.

38
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.020
Edwin Edwards, "Oh I knew your brother."

39
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.940
Everybody knew each other in Mossville because it wasn't that big not to know everybody.
